\f1\fs22 Alexander Shakti's private jet touched down at a small airstrip ninety kilometres from the city, where no customs officials would document his return. The officially sanctioned narrative\'97that he remained on extended honeymoon in Europe with occasional business meetings in Milan\'97needed to persist a while longer.\
He adjusted his designer sunglasses in the back of the nondescript sedan his assistant had arranged. The last six weeks had granted him clarity, if not peace. Between the spurious treatment centers and meditation retreats that constituted his "exile," Alexander had pieced together fragments of information: Sarah's accelerating pregnancy, the strange activities at Sublime Point, and most disturbing, the three "Sages" who had insinuated themselves into Sarah's orbit.\
"Something's not right with those old men," he muttered, reviewing their dossiers on his tablet. His research team had compiled impressively thin files, considering the Sages' apparent influence.\
"Sir?" His driver glanced in the rearview mirror.\
"Nothing." Alexander swiped through the meager information. "Take me to the Cloudborne District. I'm not ready to announce my return to the family just yet."\
His mother would be furious, of course. Eloise Shakti had arranged his "treatment program" with the precision of a military operation and the compassion of a calculator. But Alexander had developed one useful skill during his years as an aimless media personality: he knew how to disappear when attention became inconvenient.\
\
Alexander's temporary headquarters occupied the penthouse of an unremarkable building owned by a Shakti shell corporation. The space had been hastily converted into a command center, with screens displaying surveillance feeds, social media chatter, and real-time tracking of key individuals.\
"What do we have on the Sages?" Alexander demanded, tossing his jacket onto a chair.\
His head researcher, a former tabloid journalist named Mai, gestured toward the central display. "They appeared approximately eleven weeks ago, initially making contact with Sarah at a public event. From there, they essentially vanished from public view."\
"Vanished?"\
"Completely. Last confirmed sighting was security footage from Travelator Station where they were collected by Dr. Katherine Logan and an unidentified Elvi driver." Mai highlighted the grainy footage. "After that, nothing. No credit card transactions, no phone activity, no social media. It's like they ceased to exist."\
"Or they went somewhere off the grid," Alexander mused. "Sublime Point?"\
"Most likely. Your mother's mountain fortress has no external surveillance feeds we can access."\
Alexander paced the room, fingers drumming against his thigh. "What about their backgrounds? Origins? Professional histories?"\
"That's where it gets strange," Mai admitted. "Their digital footprints only go back about fourteen months. Before that\'97nothing. Their credentials are impeccable but untraceable. The universities they claim to have taught at have records, but no colleagues who remember them clearly."\
"Fabricated identities," Alexander concluded. "Professional enough to withstand casual scrutiny."\
"Exactly. And there's more." Mai brought up a series of ancient text fragments. "They publish occasional academic papers on obscure philosophical topics, primarily focused on something called 'The Ephesian Prophecy'\'97a supposedly lost ancient Greek text about cosmic rebirth."\
Alexander froze. "Cosmic rebirth?"\
"Yes. It references a 'vessel' and a 'new Dawkins' that will emerge during a 'convergence of worlds.'" Mai glanced up. "Mean anything to you?"\
Alexander's jaw tightened. "It means someone is manipulating my wife and unborn child for their own agenda."\
He turned toward the window, gazing up at the distant outline of Sublime Point Mountain against the evening sky. "I need eyes on those men. Set up surveillance on all approaches to Sublime Point. If they leave, I want to know immediately."\
\
Three days of monitoring produced nothing until a vehicle signature matched a luxury car leaving Sublime Point's service road after midnight.\
"Movement," Mai announced, activating Alexander's comm. "Single vehicle, three male occupants matching Sage profiles."\
Alexander, who had been dozing in the surveillance van, instantly came alert. "Direction?"\
"Citybound. Looks like they're heading downtown."\
"Follow, but maintain distance. And wake up the camera crew."\
An hour later, Alexander watched from across the street as the three men entered an establishment called Agora Symposium Bar.\
"Greek themed," he noted. "Fitting."\
The camera operator, a perpetually unimpressed woman named Devi who had filmed most of Candid Criminal's infamous episodes, hefted her equipment. "What's the angle here, boss? Three old guys having drinks isn't exactly ratings gold."\
"Trust me," Alexander replied, adjusting his microphone. "These aren't ordinary old men."\
Using connections that only Shakti money could buy, Alexander secured the private dining booth adjacent to the Sages' VIP area. A jammer prevented their conversation from being recorded directly, but Alexander had anticipated this. He dispatched a server\'97generously bribed\'97to place a specialized listening device disguised as an olive pit among their appetizers.\
As the Sages' conversation filtered through his earpiece, Alexander's expression transformed from concentration to disbelief to something approaching horror.\
"Are you getting this?" he whispered to Devi, who nodded, capturing his reactions.\
The Sages' discussion of prophecies, dimensional barriers, and universal dissolution confirmed Alexander's worst suspicions. When they mentioned Sarah, the discs, and a child "born into a universe of his own making," his hands gripped the table edge until his knuckles whitened.\
"They're planning something catastrophic," he whispered. "Using Sarah and my son as catalysts."\
When the philosophical debate erupted into physical confrontation, Alexander signaled Devi to keep filming. The absurdity of three elderly academics engaged in ancient Greek wrestling while discussing the end of reality wasn't lost on him.\
"This is it," he murmured as security escorted the disheveled Sages from the premises. "The perfect final episode."\
Devi lowered her camera. "Final episode of what?"\
Alexander's smile was sharp and sudden. "Candid Criminal: Apocalypse Edition."\
In the van returning to his headquarters, Alexander stared at the footage, mind racing. This wasn't just about exposing the Sages or their manipulation of Sarah. This was bigger\'97reality-altering bigger.\
"We'll need more crews," he announced. "Multiple teams, positioned throughout Sublime Point when we make our move."\
"When will that be?" Mai asked.\
Alexander checked his watch. "According to our ancient wrestling philosophers, we have until midnight on December 25th when 'the barriers dissolve.' That gives us three days to prepare the most important media event in history."\
He leaned back, a plan crystallizing. "Candid Criminal began by exposing the absurdities of everyday life. It will end by documenting the transformation of reality itself."\
Left unspoken was his deeper motivation: to save Sarah and his unborn son from whatever cosmic scheme the Sages had ensnared them in. For perhaps the first time in his privileged life, Alexander Shakti was thinking of someone other than himself.
